# Customer Churn — SQL ETL, cost-sensitive prediction, retention targeting

Telecom churn analysis rebuilt from a joint SQL / Power BI / Python project into a
reproducible pipeline: a DuckDB ETL anyone can re-run from a clone, honest baselines, a
cost-optimal decision threshold, a FastAPI scoring service, a Streamlit dashboard, Docker
and CI.

> **Attribution.** The original analysis was joint work with a course partner. The source
> notebook, the five SQL Server scripts and the Power BI report are preserved unchanged in
> `notebooks/`, `sql/original/` and `powerbi/`. This repository is the rebuild: the DuckDB
> port, the baselines, the threshold optimisation, the serving layer and the test suite are new.

---

## The finding

The original project reported **84% accuracy** from a Random Forest. Two references it
never computed change what that number means:

**Predicting "nobody churns" scores 71.2%**, because that is the base rate. The model's
real improvement is 12.8 points, not 84.

**A one-line rule — flag every month-to-month contract — recovers 88.3% of churners** at
52.4% precision, contacting 48.6% of the base. The Random Forest at its default 0.5
threshold recovered **65%**. On the metric a retention team is measured on, the
if-statement won by 23 points.

That is not an argument against the model. It reframes the problem: the model's job is to
**match the rule's reach while contacting far fewer customers**, which is a threshold and
precision problem, not an accuracy problem. Everything in `src/customer_churn/evaluate.py`
follows from that.

Full write-up: **[`reports/data_audit.md`](reports/data_audit.md)**.

## What the pipeline does differently

| Original | Here | Why |
|---|---|---|
| SSMS import wizard + 5 scripts run by hand | `make etl` rebuilds every table and view in DuckDB from the CSV | A clone reproduces the database in one command |
| `LabelEncoder` fitted before the split | `ColumnTransformer` inside the pipeline | The category vocabulary was being learned from test rows |
| Ordinal codes for `State`, `Payment_Method` | One-hot | Those columns have no order |
| Threshold 0.5 | Threshold minimising expected cost | 0.5 is a default, not a decision |
| Accuracy | Recall, precision, PR-AUC, Brier, expected cost — all against baselines | Accuracy at a 28.8% base rate is close to uninformative |
| No calibration | Isotonic, with the ROC-AUC check that it did not reorder | The cost minimum sits in the wrong place if scores are miscalibrated |

## Cost model

The threshold is chosen by minimising

```
cost = (customers contacted x intervention cost) + (churners missed x value lost)
```

with an assumed 8:1 ratio in `config/config.yaml`. That ratio is an estimate, so
`reports/cost_sensitivity.csv` re-derives the optimal threshold across ratios from 2:1 to
20:1 — if the recommendation is fragile, the sweep shows it rather than hiding it.

## Quickstart

```bash
make setup     # venv + deps + editable install
make etl       # build data/processed/churn.duckdb from the CSV via sql/
make audit     # reproduce every figure in reports/data_audit.md (pandas only)
make train     # baselines, leaderboard, threshold, calibration, artefacts
make test      # pytest + coverage
make api       # FastAPI on :8000  (/docs)
make app       # Streamlit on :8501
docker compose up
```

On Windows there is no `make`; use the PowerShell wrapper instead — `.\tasks.ps1 setup`, `.\tasks.ps1 train`, `.\tasks.ps1 test`. See [`RUNME.md`](RUNME.md).

## Outputs

| File | Contents |
|---|---|
| `reports/data_audit.json` | Machine-readable audit; regenerated in CI |
| `reports/baselines.csv` | The four no-model reference strategies |
| `reports/leaderboard.csv` | Every model at 0.5 and at its cost-optimal threshold |
| `reports/threshold_curve.csv` | Expected cost, recall and reach across thresholds |
| `reports/cost_sensitivity.csv` | How the threshold moves with the assumed cost ratio |
| `reports/predictions.md` | Three pre-registered predictions and whether each held |
| `reports/summary.json` | Winning model, threshold, held-out scores |

## SQL

`sql/` holds the DuckDB port; `sql/original/` holds the untouched SQL Server scripts.

| Script | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `01_staging.sql` | Load the CSV into `stg_churn` + a row-count assertion view |
| `02_profile.sql` | Distribution views, plus `churn_rate_by_contract` |
| `03_nullcheck.sql` | 32 hand-written null counters collapsed to one `UNPIVOT` |
| `04_production.sql` | `prod_churn` with `ISNULL` → `COALESCE` |
| `05_views.sql` | `vw_churn_data` (modelling) / `vw_join_data` (scoring) |

`tests/test_sql.py` asserts the port is faithful: staging matches the CSV row for row, no
targeted null survives, the two views partition the customers exactly, and the modelling
view excludes the outcome columns.

## API

| Method | Route | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/health` | Liveness, artefact presence |
| GET | `/metadata` | The model's measured scores |
| POST | `/predict` | One customer → probability, decision, threshold |
| POST | `/predict/batch` | Up to 5,000 customers |

`/predict` also returns `rule_baseline_would_flag`, so a caller can see when the model and
the one-line rule disagree — the only cases where the model changes a decision.

## Power BI

`powerbi/Churn Analysis Project.pbix` is the descriptive dashboard, kept as a binary
artefact because Power BI Desktop is Windows-only and cannot run in CI. `powerbi/README.md`
explains how to re-point it at the DuckDB views, and why `Churn_Category` / `Churn_Reason`
are legitimate there but banned from the model.

## Layout

```
sql/                    DuckDB port (+ original/ SQL Server scripts)
src/customer_churn/
  config.py             typed config; rejects degenerate cost settings
  etl.py                DuckDB runner + staging sanity assertions
  audit.py              every figure in reports/data_audit.md; pandas-only
  baselines.py          majority class, contact-everyone, and two rules
  features.py           encoding + the leakage guard
  models.py             model zoo + optional calibration wrapper
  evaluate.py           metrics, expected cost, threshold sweep, sensitivity
  train.py              orchestration; writes all reports
app/                    FastAPI service + 4-tab Streamlit dashboard
tests/                  baselines, leakage guard, cost arithmetic, SQL port, API
notebooks/, powerbi/    original artefacts, unmodified
```

